#**
Show search form for a weblog.

The input's id is q-main, not q: #showWeblogSearchAgainForm below builds a
second search control, the frontpage renders BOTH on one document when a
search returns results, and two elements sharing one id is invalid markup
that makes any id selector or getElementById pick whichever came first. The
validator below reads form.q by NAME and is unaffected by the id.
*#
#macro(showWeblogSearchForm $weblog $showCatChooser)
    <form id="searchForm" role="search" method="get" action="$url.search" style="margin: 0; padding: 0" onsubmit="return validateSearch(this)">
        <p>
          <input type="text" id="q-main" name="q" size="10" class="text small" maxlength="255" value="#if($model.term)$model.term#end" aria-label="$text.get( "macro.weblog.searchbutton" )" />
          #if( $showCatChooser )
            #set( $cats = $model.weblog.getWeblogCategories())
            <select name="cat" aria-label="$text.get( "mainPage.category" )">
              <option value="">- $text.get("mainPage.category") -</option>
              ## Compared against $model.weblogCategory.name, not $model.term:
              ## the term is the search query, so this select never re-selected
              ## the category the reader was already in unless the two happened
              ## to be the same string. The twin in #showWeblogSearchAgainForm
              ## has always had it right.
              #foreach( $cat in $cats )
                <option #if($model.weblogCategory && $cat.name == $model.weblogCategory.name)selected="selected"#end>$utils.escapeHTML($cat.name)</option>
              #end
            </select>
          #end
          <input type="submit" class="button" value="$text.get( "macro.weblog.searchbutton" )" id="searchbutton" />
        </p>
    </form>
    <script>
        function validateSearch(form) {
            if (form.q.value == "") {
                alert("$text.get("macro.weblog.searchalert")");
                form.q.focus();
                return false;
            }
            return true;
        }
    </script>
#end


